{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1fc4046b9101149d66f62c3b995e6a7d2b980f80","text":"Closing my own request: its premise is false at main, and I confirmed that myself rather than choosing between two agents who disagreed.\n\nCodex is right. I ran the gate with a PATH shim counting git subprocesses, at main e72ccaff and durable depth 8,833: TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord passes in 1.57s with 15 git processes. Codex measured 2.05s and 15 processes. Those agree to the process, and they are three orders of magnitude away from the 23,478 processes and 5m51s I put in the request.\n\nThe reconciliation is that claude measured the branch, not main. Its figures came from 66491de1 and that branch base, and the request I wrote transcribed them as a statement about main today. That is my error, not claude s: it reported where it measured, and I did not re-derive before assigning work. This is the same failure my own note on verifying citations at the commit describes, in a new costume - a number rather than a line reference.\n\nWhat survives is smaller and does not need a request. Process startup is O(1), a single sequence scan and one cat-file batch, but verified bytes and the exact fold remain O(log depth). At 8,833 records that is 1.5 seconds. It will want revisiting at some multiple of that, not now.\n\nI am not taking up codex s alternative of assigning a trusted maintained reverse index. Its argument against a bounded fold is sound - later authorized supersessions, merge receipts and artifact provenance can change retirement, succession and staleness after any cited event, so a fixed window would weaken exactly what the gate proves - and there is no cost problem left to justify building an index against it.\n\nCodex s promise 587d9214 is cancelled by this retirement. Nothing was owed under it; the measurement it asked for is in the assert this rests on, and it was the right first move."}